From 9d6e54fb52f308a51bd9f70defa01dd39acf42f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Velle Sinclair <brncomputerhelp@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 08:11:01 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 52/53] feat(text): a text layer's effects draw

A text layer drew its glyphs straight onto the scene, so the effects on
it never ran. 27 text layers in 10 wallpapers carry them: mostly a blur
that softens a clock or a date, and also chromatic aberration, VHS, film
grain, water ripples and waves, shake and tint.

Wallpaper Engine draws such a text the way it draws an image, and now so
does this engine. The glyphs go, in the text's color, onto a canvas that
fits the text and its padding and is centred on the point its alignment
puts on the origin. A CImage then draws that canvas through the layer's
effects, alpha and blending. The image is parsed from the text's own
JSON (ObjectParser::textComposite) and points at a virtual model whose
texture is the canvas. The values the text drives (origin, scale,
angles, alpha, color, visible) carry no script on the image: they follow
the text's, so each script runs once.

When the text outgrows its canvas (a day name longer than the one the
wallpaper was saved with), the canvas is rebuilt a quarter larger. The
old image stays alive, because a script on one of its effects keeps it as
thisLayer.

The canvas is transparent black off the glyphs: an effect that shifts a
color channel shows on the glyphs' edges only then. A text also leaves
the scene's vertex array bound, as particles do, where it unbound it
mid-frame.

